\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Unsurprisingly, Ezekiel chapter 18 begins by signaling a new oracle and how God gave it through his prophet with the usual formula, 'the word of the LORD came to me'. More surprisingly, the oracle then dispenses with other structural and formulaic elements to quote and interrogate a proverb from the people about the land of Israel: 'Parents eat sour grapes, and the children's teeth are dulled'.\
This immediately gives us an interpretive challenge. A proverb like this is a metaphor requiring careful analysis of the rhetoric of the whole oracle and the position of the people, as the words of the proverb itself lend themselves to multiple interpretations. The physical reality at the heart of the proverb is clear enough - some grapes are unripe, and they pucker the mouth and give an odd sensation in the teeth of the eater. The parents and the children are likely to be rather literal. The question then is, what is the eating, and what is the unpleasant sensation.\
The text of the Bible is where we should look for our answers to those questions.\
Before we jump into the evidence of this particular chapter though, I want us to look at the evidence from the rest of the Hebrew Bible. First, consider four similar statements in Scripture with which to compare the proverb of 18:2.\
The first is from Jeremiah, chapter 31. This quotes the same proverb, making it clear that the people tend to say it when it predicts "they shall no longer say". The wording of the proverb is slightly different: "The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge." The wording change is explainable by the prediction Jeremiah is making, "they shall no longer say". The interpretation of the original, people's version is not given directly - but it is directly opposed to "everyone shall die for his own iniquity. Each man who eats sour grapes, his teeth shall be set on edge."\
The second is from Ezekiel itself, chapter 16 verse 44: "like mother, like daughter". This is explicitly called a proverb, using the same word as in 18:2. In context, it speaks of the tendency of daughters to act as do their mothers, applying that to the wicked conduct of Jerusalem. \
The third is from Lamentations, so we do not have clear evidence that it was known to Ezekiel, and he very likely is not drawing on it. It is not explicitly labeled a proverb, and it probably is not one. But it is from the same time period, on a similar topic, with a similar metaphor. Lamentations 5:7, "Our fathers sinned, and are no more;\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0and we bear their iniquities." While the genre and wording are different, this may well reflect the same proverb in popular currency. Lamentations may or may not be doing the same thing with it as Ezekiel, but both its use and the use of the proverb in the wider culture ought to be taken into account.\
The fourth is from Ezekiel itself as well, the metaphor at the heart of chapter 17. A vine is moved, then it stretches itself unwisely, then it is uprooted - while a sprout is also taken and planted, but it flourishes. This is called a proverb - but it is also called a riddle. It is much longer, and it is not nearly so similar in theme. However, generational descent is still somewhat in view, and the generations are those of the kings of Judah.\
Next, consider principles from the Torah which this chapter may be in dialogue with.\
First, from Genesis 9, "And for your lifeblood I will require a reckoning: from every beast I will require it and from man. From his fellow man I will require a reckoning for the life of man. Whoever sheds the blood of man, by man shall his blood be shed, for God made man in his own image." The claim that the lives of all humans belong to God because humans, male and female, were made in the image of God, goes back to Genesis 1.\
Second, from Exodus 20 and elsewhere, "I the Lord your God am a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers on the children to the third and fourth generation of those who hate me". The LORD refuses to accept the people he loves going after idols, and even their children to the fourth generation will suffer if they do something that unacceptable - as was the case for Manasseh, which is relevant as the 3rd and 4th generation in descent from him suffered partially because of his idolatry.\
Third, from Deuteronomy 24, human administered justice is to be impartial. The weak are not to receive less justice - see Deuteronomy 24:17. Bias because of the crimes of parents similarly has no place in human justice - \'93Fathers shall not be put to death because of their children, nor shall children be put to death because of their fathers. Each one shall be put to death for his own sin."\
Lastly, we have the evidence of Ezekiel 18. I would like to propose that the rhetoric of the chapter should be interpreted as if it is coherent. That is, the rhetoric directly opposed to that proverb should make sense as opposition to it, and that opposition should fit with the rhetoric of the entire chapter.\
So, what did the people mean by the proverb, and how was Ezekiel instructed to oppose that meaning?\
There is, of course, a well known interpretation. I'll let you speak to it - but only as a hypothesis in need of confirmation. The text may offer that confirmation, or the text may say otherwise.
