\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Say we consider those three hypotheses, confusion about God's justice, inevitability and fatalistic despair, and cynical deflection of responsibility.\
Under each theory for what was meant by the people when they used that proverb, what then is the role of the steps in Ezekiel's responding rhetoric? The oath that the proverb will not be used, the claim that all lives are the LORD's, the claim that the sinful life will end in death, the following casuistry?\
Having answered that, which theory best fits the responding rhetoric?
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Opus:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Looking at how Ezekiel's rhetoric responds to each hypothesis:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 If confusion about divine justice:
\b0 \
The oath (v.3) would be establishing divine authority to clarify what seemed contradictory - not overturning the principle but correcting misapplication. "All lives are mine" (v.4a) clarifies that God has direct relationship with each generation, not mediated through ancestors - the father's soul and son's soul equally belong to God. "The soul that sins shall die" (v.4b) reaffirms Deuteronomy's principle at divine level. The casuistry then demonstrates how this works across generations, showing that what appears to be inherited punishment is actually each generation's own choices bearing fruit.\
The rhetoric here would be pedagogical - walking through scenarios to untangle legitimate confusion about how individual and collective judgment interact.\

\b If fatalistic despair:
\b0 \
The oath becomes a breaking of perceived inevitability - God swearing this pattern will cease. "All lives are mine" asserts divine ownership that transcends generational chains - God can claim any soul regardless of lineage. "The soul that sins shall die" paradoxically becomes hope: death is tied to sin, not birth. The casuistry, especially verses 21-23 about the wicked turning, directly attacks fatalism by demonstrating possibility of change in either direction.\
The rhetoric would be liberation - breaking perceived chains of inherited doom.\

\b If cynical deflection:
\b0 \
The oath is forceful termination of an excuse - God will no longer tolerate this deflection. "All lives are mine" removes the buffer of ancestors - you can't hide behind your fathers when God owns you directly. "The soul that sins shall die" strips away the deflection to make responsibility immediate and personal. The casuistry then systematically demolishes every variation of the excuse, showing that righteousness and wickedness can't be inherited as shields or burdens.\
The rhetoric would be confrontational - stripping away pretense.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The elaborateness of the casuistry seems key - it's not just stating a principle but working through multiple permutations. This suggests the proverb represented something more complex than simple deflection. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Which fits best?
\b0 \
The fatalistic despair hypothesis best explains the rhetorical strategy. Several elements point this way:\
First, the elaborate demonstration that change is possible in both directions (verses 21-24) seems unnecessarily complex if merely correcting confusion or confronting deflection. But if fighting despair about inherited corruption, showing bi-directional change is essential.\
Second, verse 23 - "Have I any pleasure in the death of the wicked?" - addresses emotional state more than intellectual error. God isn't just correcting wrong thinking but responding to despair about divine disposition.\
Third, the climax in verses 31-32 - "Cast away from you all your transgressions... and make yourselves a new heart and a new spirit! Why will you die, O house of Israel?" - presumes they're choosing death despite available life. This fits those trapped in fatalistic thinking who've given up trying.\
Fourth, the promise of new heart and spirit specifically addresses the "like mother, like daughter" problem - if corruption is inherited, only divine intervention can break the pattern.\
The confusion hypothesis fails because the rhetoric isn't primarily clarifying but commanding change. The deflection hypothesis fails because the tone shifts from confrontation to pleading - God seems genuinely distressed that they're choosing death, not just angry at their excuses.\
The proverb represented theological despair weaponized - they used inherited guilt to justify continued rebellion, believing change impossible. Ezekiel's response attacks both the despair (change is possible) and its weaponization (you're responsible for choosing change or death).
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
