\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While some of that despair was clearly theological, incorrect theology that is, Ezekiel has also had a strain of critique of practical atheism. Ezekiel has preached against thinking and acting as if God will not act, with the false implications that doom comes from the balance of human forces against one's nation and God angering actions to attain material advantage are a sensible course. Your messages from previous instances on previous chapters ought to give some of the flavor of that critique. That practical atheism might be contributing to the sense of inevitable doom. If one's fathers brought about a world situation where one's nation is outnumbered and attacked, what is there to be done about it?\
However, before adopting that practical atheism as part of our interpretation, the evidence for it would need to be carefully considered.\
The three illustrations of Ezekiel's counter to the proverb, which are phrased as case law, speak against this practical atheism as well. That is, practical atheism is one way of looking at the wicked behavior condemned in them. These illustrations are complex enough that there can be multiple influences, multiple threads running throughout, and multiple messages in them. \
This leads to another interpretive problem, one with potentially multiple answers. Are the lists of righteous and wicked behavior in these illustrations examples of personal morality? Religious purity? A broader royal standard of behavior? A commentary on the recent kings of Judah and their generational sin? Leviticus 18 style uncleanliness which makes unclean a land so that the land vomits out its inhabitants? A commentary on Jeremiah 22?\
There is at least some evidence for each of these - the question is whether the resemblance is accidental, or if it is intentional, widespread, and linked into the larger rhetoric of the chapter (was it would be if intentional).
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Opus:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 <notes> The previous instances emphasized: - Chapter 7: "The most dangerous assumption... is that divine non-intervention is permanent because it's been long" - Chapter 12: Temporal deflection - categorizing prophecy as distant rather than immediate - Chapter 13: False prophets providing theological cover for continuation of harm </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The practical atheism thread strengthens the interpretation significantly. If previous generations created impossible political circumstances and God seems absent, the proverb becomes: "Our fathers ate the sour grapes [made the disastrous political choices], and our teeth are set on edge [we face inevitable doom from superior enemies]." This isn't just theological fatalism but practical despair masquerading as theology.\
Looking at the behavioral lists through this lens:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Evidence for each interpretive frame:
\b0 \
The lists clearly incorporate 
\b personal morality
\b0  - feeding the hungry, clothing the naked. But these appear alongside explicitly judicial functions like "true judgment between parties" (
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1463 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1502 \u1462 \u1514 
\f1 , v.8), suggesting something beyond individual ethics.\

\b Religious purity
\b0  is prominent - eating on mountains (idolatrous cult meals), lifting eyes to gillulim (dung-idols). But these religious violations interweave with economic crimes, suggesting the idolatry itself might be practically motivated - seeking divine favor for material advantage.\
The 
\b royal standard
\b0  evidence is stronger than initially apparent. "True judgment between parties" is explicitly governmental. The usury prohibition and pledge-return requirements regulate economic power. The comprehensiveness itself - covering religious, judicial, economic, sexual spheres - matches royal responsibility for national righteousness.\

\b Commentary on Judean kings
\b0  gains support from specific parallels. Manasseh shed innocent blood and filled Jerusalem with idols. Jehoiakim (Jeremiah 22) built through oppression and withheld wages. The alternating righteous/wicked/righteous pattern in the casuistry could mirror Josiah/Jehoiakim/Jehoiachin.\

\b Leviticus 18 parallels
\b0  are striking. Sexual violations, bloodshed, and idolatry all appear - the exact triad that defiles land until it vomits out inhabitants. Verse 6's "approach a menstruating woman" directly echoes Leviticus 18:19. This suggests the sins aren't random but specifically those that make the land uninhabitable.\

\b Jeremiah 22 resonances
\b0  are substantial. Both texts combine bloodshed, oppression of poor, unjust gain, and judicial corruption. Both address rulers/leaders. Jeremiah 22:3 demands "do justice and righteousness" - Ezekiel 18:5 uses nearly identical language (
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1464 \u1474 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1511 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 ).\

\b Which frame is primary?
\b0 \
The evidence suggests these aren't alternative readings but layered meanings. The lists function as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Land-defiling sins
\b0  (Leviticus 18 frame) that explain why exile was inevitable\
\ls1\ilvl0
\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Royal failures
\b0  (Jeremiah 22/kings frame) showing how leaders created current circumstances\
\ls1\ilvl0
\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practical atheism symptoms
\b0  - each violation represents choosing immediate advantage over covenant faithfulness\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The brilliance is that Ezekiel demonstrates these very sins that previous generations used to "eat sour grapes" (gain temporary advantage through covenant violation) are still available to the current generation. They could oppress, take usury, pursue idols for political advantage. But verse 21 - the wicked can turn and live - means they can also refuse these options.\
The practical atheism interpretation explains why God insists "Cast away your transgressions... make yourselves a new heart" (v.31). If they believe human circumstances determine fate, they'll repeat their fathers' pragmatic violations. Only recognizing God's immediate sovereignty enables breaking the cycle.\
The comprehensive nature of the lists isn't accidental but essential to the argument: every sphere where previous kings sought advantage through violation (religious apostasy for divine favor, economic oppression for resources, sexual alliances for political security) remains under divine sovereignty. The same choices that seemed practical necessities to their fathers are still sins that bring death.\
This reframes the entire chapter: not just about individual versus collective responsibility, but about whether divine sovereignty operates in practical reality or only theological abstraction. The proverb represented surrender to human causation; Ezekiel insists divine causation remains primary and immediate.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
