\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As further evidence for pursuing idols being for political advantage, the phrase 'lift up his eyes' applied to the idols is used Ezekiel 23 with respect to metaphorical lewdness and whoring, clearly about seeking advantage, specifically in reference to Egypt. However, the base meaning of 'lift up his eyes' does not really say what kind of aid or advantage is sought - we must infer that political advantage is part of the advantage sought.\
Also, we've not really examined in detail yet how these lists of wicked and righteous behavior fit into the specific rhetorical moves of verse 4. The death penalty, presumably at God's hands, in verse 4 is mirrored in verses 13 and 18. But also, the claim that all lives are the LORD's runs throughout the three cases.\
