\cf2 This reading explains why these specific sins appear repeatedly across Ezekiel. They're not arbitrary moral failures but the practical manifestations of forgetting that human lives belong to God.\
The "lifting up eyes" to idols fits perfectly here. If you truly believed all lives belonged to YHWH, you wouldn't seek other divine powers for advantage. But if you're operating from practical atheism - treating human life as mere resource while seeking whatever divine or political backing gives advantage - then pursuing idols for political favor makes strategic sense.\
This also illuminates why the chapter's rhetoric keeps returning to life and death. The sins aren't just "wrong" in abstract moral terms; they're specifically violations of divine ownership that result in death because they deny the source of life itself. The wicked son "shall not live" (
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1495 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1 , v.13) not arbitrarily but because his actions systematically deny the divine ownership that sustains life.\
The practical atheism becomes even clearer: if you act as though human lives are just resources for exploitation rather than divine property, you're effectively denying God's active sovereignty. The political advantages sought through idolatry, the economic gains through oppression, the power maintained through injustice - all assume human causation determines outcomes rather than divine ownership of every life involved.\
This reframes the proverb's fatalism. If our fathers treated lives as expendable resources for political maneuvering, creating impossible circumstances, what can we do? Ezekiel's answer: recognize that every life still belongs to God, that divine sovereignty still operates, and that respecting divine ownership of human life (through the listed behaviors) remains the path to life regardless of inherited circumstances.\
The three cases then demonstrate that this recognition can emerge or disappear in any generation. The righteous father who recognizes divine ownership can have a son who forgets it. The wicked father who ignored it can have a son who remembers. Neither recognition nor blindness is inherited - each generation chooses whether to acknowledge that all lives are God's.
