\b \cf2 Potential problems:
\b0 \
The 
\b ritual/cultic violations
\b0  create tension. "Eating on the mountains" and approaching a menstruating woman are ritual impurities that don't obviously connect to contempt for human life or practical atheism. They might fit the land-defilement layer (Leviticus 18), but their prominence suggests they're not just included for completeness.\
The 
\b distinctive phrasing around covenant law
\b0  - "walked in my statutes and kept my ordinances to act faithfully" (v.9) - frames righteousness as comprehensive covenant faithfulness, not just avoiding specific categories of sin. This holistic language resists reduction to our three layers.\
The 
\b immediacy of judgment
\b0  language - "he shall surely live/die" - seems present-tense, not about past exile or future restoration. The exiles already lost the land, so warning about land-defilement seems retrospective rather than motivational.\

\b Most challenging:
\b0  The 
\b repentance section
\b0  (vv.21-32) abandons the specific sin lists entirely. It speaks abstractly about "all his transgressions" and "all my statutes," suggesting the specific sins were illustrative rather than exhaustive. This universalizing move doesn't fit neatly into our layers.\

\b Testing our interpretation against rhetorical structure:
\b0 \
The chapter's rhetoric moves from specific (case law) to universal (any wicked can turn). This progression suggests the cases establish a principle that transcends their specific content. Our layered reading explains the specific content well but might miss why Ezekiel moves beyond it.\
Verses 25 and 29 - "The way of the Lord is not balanced" - interrupt with the people's complaint. But nothing in the case law seems unbalanced; the imbalance appears when discussing the possibility of change (vv.21-24). This suggests the real issue isn't the inherited guilt but the perceived unfairness of forgetting past righteousness or wickedness.\

\b A deeper problem:
\b0 \
Verse 30 - "I will judge each of you according to his ways" - uses 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1512 \u1499 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  (ways/paths), not the specific sins listed. Similarly, verse 23 asks if God desires the death of the wicked, not the death of the oppressor or idolater. The rhetoric consistently abstracts from specific sins to general orientation.\
This abstraction might reveal that our layers, while present, serve a different function. Rather than being the main point, they might be demonstrating what "ways" look like in practice. The specific sins show what it means to walk in ways that acknowledge or deny divine sovereignty.\

\b Refining the interpretation:
\b0 \
The counterevidence doesn't destroy our reading but suggests refinement. The layers aren't separate themes but manifestations of a single reality: choosing to operate within or outside divine sovereignty.\
