\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Eating on the mountains is difficult to interpret. However, it does occur later in Ezekiel, so we have some hints. Ezekiel 22 has a similar list of sins with that same phrase:\
"Behold, the princes of Israel in you, every one according to his power, have been bent on shedding blood.\'a0Father and mother are treated with contempt in you; the sojourner suffers extortion in your midst; the fatherless and the widow are wronged in you.\'a0You have despised my holy things and profaned my Sabbaths.\'a0There are men in you who slander to shed blood, and people in you who eat on the mountains; they commit lewdness in your midst. \'a0In you men uncover their fathers' nakedness; in you they violate women who are unclean in their menstrual impurity. \'a0One commits abomination with his neighbor's wife; another lewdly defiles his daughter-in-law; another in you violates his sister, his father's daughter. \'a0In you they take bribes to shed blood; you take interest and profit and make gain of your neighbors by extortion; but me you have forgotten, declares the Lord God."\
That context also has the land defilement layer, the bloodshed, the royal failure, and forgetting the LORD. \
Also, there may be a more direct link to blood, as well as to land defilement. Ezekiel 33 makes this accusation: "You eat flesh with the blood and lift up your eyes to your idols and shed blood; shall you then possess the land?\'a0You rely on the sword, you commit abominations, and each of you defiles his neighbor's wife; shall you then possess the land?" One of the reasons Torah forbade eating meat with the blood was that the blood is connected to life. But another reason was to shut off sacrifices to things that are not God.\
Also, while exactly what was happening on the mountains is not specified, there is a link to royal failure. 2 Kings 23 seems to indicate that Josiah ended the practice. If the practice has returned, what does that say about the kings who allowed this?\
So the eating on the mountains may have links to both the land defilement layer and the royal failure layer.\
On the other hand, the covenant law phrasing is a more serious issue for our hypothesis. It explains the references to the law in two of our layers. Seen one way, that means it is not counterevidence. But seen another way, the ability to explain the very real evidence for our hypothesis by means of a different hypothesis means this is an alternative hypothesis we need to consider carefully. Some of the distinctive covenant law phrasing may come out of Ezekiel's priestly background and his reliance on Torah in previous chapters. But that phrasing is almost certainly part of his rhetorical strategy in this chapter.\
The covenant law reference is not just a matter of phrasing - the first list of righteous behavior has, at the end, 'walks in my decrees and keeps my judgements'.\
I consider the immediacy of judgement language a subset of covenant law phrasing - some of the exact phrases are part of the Torah, and the others are similar.\
I agree that the sin lists were illustrative rather than exhaustive, and that the layers really only extend as far as the sin lists. That is why it is so important to ask what role the sin lists fit in the broader rhetorical strategy of the oracle as a whole. What do they illustrate? Why that particular illustration? They are not the main point. But the particular choices of illustration may reveal things about wickedness we would not know if we went with our idea of wickedness, and they may teach us things about the rhetoric of the chapter as a whole if we pay attention to the connections.\
Similarly, it very much is the case that the rhetoric turns to the possibility of change. The wicked can indeed turn, and the righteous can indeed fall. That the rhetoric goes in this direction is part of the evidence for the inevitability and fatalistic despair hypothesis for the initial proverb of the chapter. 'Can change' is a direct counter to inevitability and fatalism. Despair is countered rather directly by 'he shall live' and the Lord implying with a question that he does not take pleasure in the death of the wicked, but rather takes pleasure in them turning and living.
