\cf2 What do these particular illustrations reveal about wickedness we wouldn't otherwise know?\
First, 
\b wickedness is systemic
\b0  - it touches worship, economics, sexuality, justice. You can't compartmentalize covenant faithfulness.\
Second, 
\b wickedness compounds
\b0  - usury leads to keeping pledges which leads to oppression. The lists show escalation patterns.\
Third, 
\b wickedness inverts creation order
\b0  - violence destroys life, oppression crushes the weak who should be protected, idolatry seeks life from non-life.\
The illustrations also reveal something crucial about the proverb's use. If these specific sins are what the fathers' "sour grapes" looked like - systematic covenant violation across every sphere of life - then the children claiming inevitable judgment aren't just inheriting consequences but are likely continuing the same patterns while claiming helplessness.\
This explains the rhetorical movement to possibility of change. Ezekiel first establishes what covenant violation looks like concretely (you can recognize it), then insists it can be abandoned (you can change), then reveals divine desire for that change (God wants you to live), and finally demands decision (why will you die?).\
The covenant law framing thus encompasses our layers while explaining their rhetorical function. The layers show what covenant breaking looks like in practice; the overall framework insists covenant relationship remains dynamic and available.\
The people use the proverb to claim they're locked into consequences of past covenant breaking. Ezekiel responds that covenant relationship is always immediate - each generation, indeed each person, stands before God's statutes and ordinances with the ability to choose. The specific sins aren't arbitrary but show what choosing death looks like in practice, making it recognizable and therefore changeable.
