\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One minor caveat - the people may not even have gotten as far as viewing the previous generation's behavior as covenant breaking. The discussion of practical atheism, here and elsewhere in Ezekiel, is a hint that their cynicism has gone that far. But that is a minor caveat to your otherwise correct point that the people are using the proverb to claim that they are locked into the consequences of the previous generation's behavior.\
On the whole though, this is a much more careful reading of the evidence, and therefore a much more solidly grounded theory - the moreso because it has begun making a serious effort to integrate the rhetoric, taking seriously what Ezekiel was trying to communicate.\
This theory also fits with the case law examples in chapter 14. There, even the most righteous of men could not save anyone but themselves, even their children. The land as a whole is doomed, by land defiling sin, by widespread rebelliousness, by continual covenant breaking. Each generation must decide for itself whether to continue in that rebelliousness and repent. There is no store of merit, transferable from father to son. Such a store of merit is falsified by this chapter - wickedness can compound, but righteousness must be continually pursued to result in life. This is made explicit in verse 20 - his righteous actions will not be remembered.\
This theory also fits with the specific discussion of mutability of consequences in verses 21 through 24. As in verse 21, repentance requires turning away from all sin and towards the LORD's decrees, resulting in practicing justice and righteousness. That repentance leads to life. As in verse 24, turning away from that righteousness towards evil leads to death.\
