\b \cf2 What the text justifies for broader application:
\b0 \
The sin lists span every sphere - economic (usury), judicial (false judgment), violent (bloodshed), sexual (violations), religious (idolatry). Ezekiel doesn't present "religious sins" and "secular sins" but patterns of covenant breaking that manifest everywhere. The chapter addresses Israel's total conduct, not their temple behavior.\
"All lives are mine" isn't limited to religious contexts. Every risk assessment treating human lives as acceptable losses, every optimization that sacrifices human welfare for efficiency, every policy discussion affecting life and death - these occur before the God who claims all lives.\
The bloodshed Ezekiel condemns includes judicial murder, economic oppression unto death, and violence. When these patterns appear in modern forms - algorithmic decisions affecting healthcare access, economic policies creating deadly poverty, military calculations treating civilian casualties as acceptable - the text's categories apply.\

\b In practice:
\b0 \
When discussions involve human life being treated as expendable for gain - whether corporate profit, political advantage, or technological progress - Ezekiel 18's framework identifies this as the same contempt for divine ownership condemned in verse 12.\
When sophisticated arguments justify inevitable harm - "market forces," "technological unemployment," "necessary casualties" - these parallel the proverb's fatalism that God forcefully rejects.\
When risk assessments abstract human death into percentages without wrestling with "all lives are mine," they demonstrate the practical atheism that acts as if only human calculations matter.\

\b What overcomes the limitation:
\b0 \
The text itself refuses compartmentalization. Verse 31's "Cast away ALL your transgressions" doesn't distinguish religious from secular. The judgment "according to your ways" (v.30) encompasses total life pattern, not religious observance.\
Most forcefully: the chapter's climax - "Why will you die?" - addresses any pattern leading to death, not just religious apostasy. Death comes through oppression, violence, and injustice as much as through idolatry.\
The sharpness of God's words applies wherever human lives are at stake. Not because I extend them but because the text's own categories transcend modern sacred/secular divisions that didn't exist for Ezekiel.
